# AQSS-36-OMEGA — Principal Architecture Review & Policy Specification

## Executive Assessment
User reinforcement must never directly modify the threat detector or its safety invariants. It should modify a bounded policy-preference layer downstream of threat inference. This transforms the system from an unstable self-modifying safety engine into a constrained adaptive controller.

### Core Architectural Flow
Sensing → Feature Extraction → Threat Inference → Safety Governor → Adaptive Policy → User Feedback

---

## P0 Core Requirements & Invariants

1. **Separation of Concerns:** Threat inference (what is happening) is strictly decoupled from user preference (how the system should respond).
2. **Safety Governor Authority:** The Safety Governor is mathematically authoritative over sensitivity. User settings cannot disable safety-critical threat detection.
3. **Transactional Policy Updates:** Direct feedback mutation is replaced with a versioned update queue and transactional commits.
4. **Bounded Learning & Hysteresis:** Incorporates clipping, mean reversion, and threshold hysteresis to prevent oscillation and calibration drift.
5. **Epistemic Humility:** Feedback states include Agree, Disagree, and Unsure (`?`).
6. **Authority Hierarchy:** 
   $$\text{Safety} > \text{System Constraints} > \text{Explicit User Command} > \text{Learned Preference} > \text{Contextual Prediction}$$

